---
permalink: /sitemap.xml
eleventyExcludeFromCollections: true
pages:
  - /
  - /who-to-pitch/
  - /what-to-send/
  - /pricing/
  - /privacy/
  - /terms/
  - /resources/
  - /help/
---
{#-
  The list of pages we want ranked — not a list of pages that exist. Search
  Console reads this as our own statement of what matters, so anything in here
  that is thin, transactional or half-rendered costs more than it earns.

  /help/ was deliberately out until 24 Aug 2026, on #135's reasoning: the
  served HTML was a shell whose entire body copy was the word "Loading…", and
  listing that is worse than omitting it. **#136 removed the reason** — the
  corpus is now prerendered at build time, so /help/ ships ~51k characters of
  real guide text and is the site's deepest long-tail content. The line #135
  left room for is the one above.

  Deliberately out, each for its own reason:
    /checkout/       a form, not an answer to any search. Left crawlable and
                     linked from the homepage CTA, so nothing is lost; if
                     Search Console ever shows it earning buy-intent
                     impressions, #137 can put it back with evidence rather
                     than on a hunch. (Decided #135, 24 Aug 2026.)
    /checkout/done/  noindex — what a buyer reads seconds after paying.
    /404.html        noindex.
    /app/            301s to /; whether that page has a future is W2's
                     question and a sitemap should not pre-empt it.

  /who-to-pitch/, /what-to-send/ and /pricing/ joined on 24 Aug 2026 (#153),
  when they stopped being anchors on the homepage and became pages. They are
  exactly what this file is for: each answers a question somebody types, each
  is the site's own best page on that question, and none of them is thin — the
  reason /checkout/ is still out is that it answers nothing.

  <lastmod>, added by #144 on exactly the terms #135 set. The static pages
  still carry none, because a build-time stamp would claim every page changed
  on every deploy and Google ignores a date it cannot trust. The articles carry
  one because theirs is real: it is the article's own published/updated date
  from its frontmatter, it moves only when the article does, and it is the
  thing a crawler should come back for.
-#}
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<urlset xmlns="http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9">
{%- for p in pages %}
  <url><loc>{{ site.url }}{{ p }}</loc></url>
{%- endfor %}
{%- for a in collections.resources | related('', 99) %}
  <url><loc>{{ site.url }}{{ a.url }}</loc><lastmod>{{ (a.data.updated or a.data.date) | isoDate }}</lastmod></url>
{%- endfor %}
</urlset>
